    {
      "id": "p0189-b012",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "The joke here is that hae aru usually means \"glorious\" or \"honorable\", but hae by itself can mean \"fly\" or \"flies\". Additionally, hae aru can be interpreted as a way of saying \"there are flies\". The result is a Japanese pun that uses completely different words to fit the scene seamlessly.",
        "raw": "The joke here is that hae arn usually means \"glorious\" or \"honorable\", but\nhae by itself can mean \"fly\" or \"flies\". Additionally, /we aru can be interpreted\nas a way of saying \"there are Aies''. The result is a Japanese pun that uses\ncompletely different words to fit the scene seamlessly.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.09299522,
          0.82617857,
          0.68571727,
          0.89395
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
